          ],
          "content": "164"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.056,
            0.057,
            0.113,
            0.074
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.056,
            0.083,
            0.205,
            0.301
          ],
          "content": "Usually in any measurement, there is uncertainity of ±1 in the last digit. Therefore, the variation in readings of digital weighing balance is within experimental error and the weight can be taken as constant."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.293,
            0.061,
            0.881,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.251,
                0.264,
                0.544,
                0.295
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.2: (b) Pouring baking soda into the conical flask containing vinegar"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.647,
            0.269,
            0.827,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 9.2: (c) Weight of the final reaction mixture"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.237,
            0.302,
            0.888,
            0.338
          ],
          "content": "6. Carefully transfer the baking soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate) from the balloon into the conical flask containing vinegar (Fig. 9.2b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.339,
            0.888,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": "7. Place the conical flask and balloon back on the weighing balance, and record the final reading (Fig. 9.2c)."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.041,
            0.415,
            0.365,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.41,
            0.38,
            0.468,
            0.396
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Note"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.041,
            0.663,
            0.363,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.11,
                0.625,
                0.355,
                0.656
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.3: (a) Weight of vinegar and baking soda"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.101,
                0.885,
                0.367,
                0.915
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 9.3: (b) Pouring baking soda into the conical flask containing vinegar"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.39,
            0.4,
            0.869,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "Keep the conical flask and the balloon on the digital weighing balance. This prevents errors caused by small traces of baking soda that may remain stuck to the balloon."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.468,
            0.619,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": "8. What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.487,
            0.802,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "9. Are the initial and the final readings same?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.508,
            0.889,
            0.559
          ],
          "content": "A brisk effervescence is observed. The final reading does not match the initial reading. What can be the reason for this?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.559,
            0.888,
            0.594
          ],
          "content": "Repeat the above experiment in a slightly modified way as explained below."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.6,
            0.607,
            0.618
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Experimental set-up 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.384,
            0.621,
            0.888,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": "1. Place a clean, dry 100 mL conical flask and a medium-sized balloon on a weighing balance."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.656,
            0.888,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": "2. Set the balance reading to zero by pressing the tare or reset button."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.691,
            0.888,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "3. Pour about 20 mL of vinegar or lemon juice into the conical flask."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.726,
            0.888,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": "4. Place about 2 g of baking soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate) in the balloon."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.761,
            0.89,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "5. Fix the balloon to the mouth of the conical flask using a thread, without allowing the baking soda to mix with the vinegar."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.812,
            0.889,
            0.864
          ],
          "content": "6. Weigh the conical flask containing vinegar and the balloon containing baking soda, and record the reading (Fig. 9.3a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.386,
            0.864,
            0.889,
            0.915
          ],
          "content": "7. Lift the other end of the balloon upwards, allowing the baking soda (sodium hydrogencarbonate) to fall into the vinegar (Fig. 9.3b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.385,
            0.916,
            0.618,
            0.934
          ],
          "content": "8. What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.945,
            0.303,
            0.963
          ],
          "content": "Exploration|Grade 9"
        }
      ]
    }
